A||||3||Cooter||IT LIVES AGAIN!!!!!||||14
Z||000000||Cooter||11-24-1999||06:22 PM||||About a week ago, the clutch slave cylinder sprung a leak and the car has been parked since then. Today, I installed the new steel clutch pedal, flushed the lines, put on the new slave cylinder, and filled it with hydraulic jack oil. You guys were right about using alternatives to brake fluid! It has never shifted this good. I did not have a helper, so I used the V8-Archie method of pushing the slave cylinder together and bleeding the air out. Anyway, so far-so good.||||reg||14
Z||000001||88formula||11-24-1999||08:48 PM||||Your useing hydraulic jack oil in yourr clutch linkage system? Tell me more and why is this better?<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-24-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||Cooter||11-24-1999||10:08 PM||||I did this because of the good luck that others have had using ATF and Mobile 1 -0w30 motor oil instead of brake fluid. The main reason is that oil doesn't attract moisture, and lubricates the leak prone slave cylinder so hopefully it will have an long, smooth life.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||11-24-1999||10:51 PM||||<BR>Hey Cooter, glad to hear we've got another convert! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
